# the Renderer and its passes: boom's core render abstraction
#
# a Renderer owns the device, the swapchain, the frame loop, the pipelines,
# and the per-frame uniform ring. a game creates one and drives each frame
# through it: renderer_begin_frame, then one or more passes, then
# renderer_end_frame to present.
#
# a Pass is a unit of drawing with a target (the window or an offscreen
# RenderTarget), a projection (perspective for 3D, orthographic for 2D),
# and clear state. the same Renderer and Pass machinery drives both 2D and
# 3D:
#
#   - pass_scene builds a 3D pass from a camera; pass_draw and
#     pass_draw_material submit mesh draws with a model transform.
#   - pass_overlay builds a 2D pass in pixel space; pass_draw_sprite
#     submits textured quads.
#   - set PassDesc.target to render into a texture, which is how offscreen
#     and post-process passes are expressed.
#
# WHAT CHANGED FROM GL, AND WHY THE API DID TOO.
#
# The Renderer presents. Under GL the engine loop swapped the window after
# the app drew, so several renderers could composite into one frame. A
# Vulkan frame is bracketed by an acquire and a present around one command
# buffer, and that bracket is the renderer's. Compositing several renderers
# is now several passes, or an offscreen target sampled by a later pass.
#
# There is no per-pass or per-material shader. A pipeline bakes both stages
# and the whole fixed-function state into one immutable object, so choosing
# a shader is choosing a pipeline. The renderer owns the ones the engine
# needs, and a game that wants its own supplies a boom.graphics.Shader and
# draws with pass_draw_shaded. The pipelines that needs are built under the
# Shader, keyed by render-pass compatibility and the vertex layout each draw
# actually uses, so a game never names a Pipeline or a RenderPass. Its shading
# parameters go in the block set by pass_set_user, which arrives at binding 4.
#
# A 3D draw takes a uniform slot. GL set named uniforms on the bound program
# between draws; a Vulkan draw reads a buffer range that must already hold
# its values when the command buffer executes, so each mesh draw writes its
# own slot.
#
# 2D draws do not, and that is deliberate. A sprite carrying a per-draw
# uniform put a hard ceiling on how many could be drawn in a frame, and the
# draws refused when it was reached were whichever came last, which in a game
# that composites an offscreen scene means the composite and the UI. So
# sprites, text and immediate-mode geometry are appended to a per-pass batch,
# expanded into the frame's vertex arena, and drawn as one call per texture
# run holding one slot. See boom.graphics.batch and boom.graphics.stream.

